              "attachment_id": null,
              "author": "sky@mozilla.com",
              "bug_id": 2038886,
              "count": 3,
              "creation_time": "2026-05-13T14:26:31Z",
              "creator": "sky@mozilla.com",
              "id": 18081873,
              "is_private": false,
              "raw_text": "This might be caused by them pushState'ing over the anchor? Chrome seems to store the anchor (or the highlight/fragment) despite it no longer being in the URL.",
              "reactions": {},
              "tags": [],
              "text": "This might be caused by them pushState'ing over the anchor? Chrome seems to store the anchor (or the highlight/fragment) despite it no longer being in the URL.",
              "time": "2026-05-13T14:26:31Z"
            },
            {
              "attachment_id": null,
              "author": "sky@mozilla.com",
              "bug_id": 2038886,
              "count": 4,
              "creation_time": "2026-05-13T15:51:55Z",
              "creator": "sky@mozilla.com",
              "id": 18082150,
              "is_private": false,
              "raw_text": "Hm, interesting, this appears to be a bit more complicated. I tried it with \n\n```html\n<script>\n  window.history.pushState({}, \"\", window.location.href);\n</script>\n<p>Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit.</p>\n```\n\nwhich shows similar behavior than Discourse does - i.e. the text isn't selected and the anchor disappears, but that testcase also breaks in Chrome, while it does *not* break in the real example linked here. So there's something else (additional?) going on.",
              "reactions": {},
              "tags": [],
              "text": "Hm, interesting, this appears to be a bit more complicated. I tried it with \n\n```html\n<script>\n  window.history.pushState({}, \"\", window.location.href);\n</script>\n<p>Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit.</p>\n```\n\nwhich shows similar behavior than Discourse does - i.e. the text isn't selected and the anchor disappears, but that testcase also breaks in Chrome, while it does *not* break in the real example linked here. So there's something else (additional?) going on.",
              "time": "2026-05-13T15:51:55Z"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
